**How to read the table** <br/>
Each row is a scenario: no control, or one of the two controls. The table shows average loss per year, the saving against no control, the assumed cost, the net benefit, the loss still undiscovered at the end of year 10, and the loss in a bad year (VaR) and in the worst 5% of years (ES).

**What the table tells you** <br/>
Both controls roughly pay for themselves on average loss alone, with net benefits of $102K and $35K a year. They also roughly halve the loss that has happened but is still unrecorded at the end of the history, from $12.6M to between $4.5M and $6.3M, and they lower loss in bad years.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
r1 <- ctl |> filter(arm == "recon_automation")
r2 <- ctl |> filter(arm == "independent_pnl_verification")
```
:::


On expected loss alone, both controls are close to break-even: reconciliation automation saves $502.30K a year against a $400.00K cost (net $102.30K), and independent P&L verification saves $735.12K against $700.00K (net $35.12K). Put differently, the most each control could cost and still break even at full effectiveness is its mean saving. The simulated 95% expected shortfall of annual loss falls from $31.8M to $26.8M and $23.4M; the intervals below show how well that is pinned down. Because baseline and control share seeds, a paired bootstrap of the *reduction* is tighter than the separate intervals: ES95 falls by $5.0M (95% interval $3.0M to $7.4M) under reconciliation automation and by $8.4M ($5.1M to $12.2M) under independent P&L verification. Both the mean and the tail savings come from the same severity-growth mechanism (see the [sensitivity](#sec-sensitivity) below).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
bind_rows(
  tail_ci |> transmute(arm, stat = "95% VaR", value = var95, lo = var95_lo, hi = var95_hi),
  tail_ci |> transmute(arm, stat = "95% ES", value = es95, lo = es95_lo, hi = es95_hi)
) |>
  mutate(arm = factor(arm, levels = names(ctl_lab))) |>
  ggplot(aes(arm, value, fill = arm)) +
  geom_col(width = 0.65) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi), width = 0.2) +
  facet_wrap(~stat) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c(baseline = "grey60", ctl_col), guide = "none") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = fmt_usd) +
  scale_x_discrete(labels = \(x) str_wrap(ctl_lab[x], 14)) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Annual loss", title = "Tail loss under each control", subtitle = "ES rests on about 100 tail years out of 2,000, so it is the noisier statistic") +
  theme_op()
```

